{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017j5Uzom8hGCEKzR1Py4nNa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan — `gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last`\n2\t\n3\tExecutable, sequential KDA optimization plan. Builds on `docs/draft.md`. Target: **A800 `sm_80`\n4\t(Ampere)**, **Triton‑only** compute, submission `solution/solution.py::run(q,k,v,state,A_log,a,\n5\tdt_bias,b,cu_seqlens,scale) -> (output, new_state)`. Budget: **100 evaluations**, tokens 1.0M soft /\n6\t1.2M hard. Evaluate only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`; `final` is\n7\toperator‑only. This turn writes the plan only — **no implementation, no evaluation**.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Guiding principles\n12\t\n13\t1. **Correctness before speed.** The first candidate is the highest‑confidence, order‑preserving\n14\t   f32 implementation. Do not trade precision for speed until a green baseline exists on all five\n15\t   workloads.\n16\t2. **One immutable idea per candidate.** Each `cNNN` is a single, frozen source version encoding one\n17\t   hypothesis. Never mutate an evaluated candidate; never reuse an ID for changed source.\n18\t3. **Spend evaluations deliberately.** Each `feedback` run consumes 1 of 100 evaluations and covers\n19\t   all five workloads. Reason on paper first; only evaluate when the hypothesis is concrete and the\n20\t   guardrail checklist (§4) passes on inspection.\n21\t4. **Change one variable at a time** once past the baseline, so each evaluation attributes a delta to\n22\t   a cause (precision knob, chunk size, block size, launch structure).\n23\t5. **Triton‑only.** No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA‑extension compute fallback. A failing Triton kernel is\n24\t   invalid and must be fixed or abandoned, never silently backed by Torch.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## 1. Solution scaffold (fixed across candidates)\n29\t\n30\t`solution/solution.py` structure that stays stable so only kernel/config changes drive new IDs:\n31\t\n32\t- `run(...)`: metadata/launch plumbing only (allowed PyTorch use).\n33\t  - Validate/normalize `scale` (`None`/`0.0 → 1/sqrt(128)`).\n34\t  - Derive `N = cu_seqlens.numel() - 1`, `T`, dtypes; allocate `output [T,8,128] bf16` and\n35\t    `new_state [N,8,128,128] f32` (zero‑initialized so empty sequences are correct by default).\n36\t  - Compute grids on device from `cu_seqlens` **without per‑sequence `.item()` host syncs** where\n37\t    possible (pass `cu_seqlens` into kernels; precompute per‑program `(seq_id, start, len)` mapping\n38\t    on device or with a single cheap host pass).\n39\t  - Handle `state is None` (zero entry state) even though all feedback workloads pass a state.\n40\t  - Launch the Triton kernel(s); return `(output, new_state)`.\n41\t- Triton kernel(s): the actual compute; this is what varies per candidate.\n42\t- Keep any autotune configs and precision constants as explicit compile‑time args so a change to\n43\t  them is a clear new candidate.\n44\t\n45\t---\n46\t\n47\t## 2. Candidate lineage strategy\n48\t\n49\tLineage is a tree rooted at the correctness anchor. Each node names parent, the single change, and\n50\tthe hypothesis. IDs are assigned strictly in evaluation order; a rejected branch does not free its ID.\n51\t\n52\t### Phase A — Correctness anchor\n53\t- **c001 (parent: none) — A0 order‑preserving f32 recurrence.**\n54\t  Grid over `(seq, v_head)` = `N×8`. State `S=[K=128,V=128]` f32 in registers/SMEM. Loop tokens of\n55\t  the sequence performing exact rank‑1 gated delta updates and `o=scale·q@S` on the inclusive state,\n56\t  in f32. GVA `head//2` indexing; V↔K transpose on load/store; empty‑seq `new_state=0`.\n57\t  **Goal:** establish that the pipeline wires up and passes tolerance on all five workloads, and\n58\t  reveal the speedup baseline scale. Expected already ≫1× vs the Python‑loop reference (no host loop,\n59\t  no per‑token launch), but low arithmetic intensity.\n60\t\n61\t### Phase B — Chunked throughput path\n62\t- **c002 (parent: c001) — A1 chunked gated delta rule (UT/WY), conservative & high‑precision.**\n63\t  Chunk `C=32`. Per (seq,head): f32 gate precompute + log‑space cumulative decay; decayed intra\n64\t  matrices; `[C,C]` UT transform solved in f32; sequential inter‑chunk state scan; intra+inter\n65\t  output. State‑touching matmuls use high precision (`input_precision=\"ieee\"` or `\"tf32x3\"`).\n66\t  **Hypothesis:** large speedup on medium/large workloads (15856e8c, 5d3fc66a) while staying within\n67\t  tolerance because summation‑order change is bounded by high‑precision matmuls. If correctness\n68\t  fails → diagnose (mask/transpose/UT) before any perf tuning; if a numerical fail, branch to smaller\n69\t  `C` (c003a) or stricter precision.\n70\t\n71\t### Phase C — Precision tuning (only after B passes)\n72\t- **c00x — precision knob sweep** on state‑touching matmuls: `ieee` → `tf32x3` → default TF32.\n73\t  One candidate per setting. **Hypothesis:** relax toward TF32 for speed as long as `new_state`\n74\t  stays within tolerance. Keep the strictest setting that still passes as the new baseline.\n75\t\n76\t### Phase D — Block/chunk tuning\n77\t- **c00x — chunk size** `C ∈ {16, 32, 64}` (one per candidate).\n78\t- **c00x — V/K blocking** `BV ∈ {64,128}` (occupancy vs tile‑size tradeoff, esp. for 5d3fc66a).\n79\t- **c00x — `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.**\n80\t  **Hypothesis:** 64 KB f32 state tile limits occupancy; smaller `BV` raises program count and helps\n81\t  the large workload; larger tiles help medium. Tune for geomean, re‑validating correctness each step.\n82\t\n83\t### Phase E — Launch structure & overhead (protect the tiny workloads)\n84\t- **c00x — fused vs pipelined** kernels (gate precompute inline vs separate pass).\n85\t- **c00x — low‑overhead path for tiny sequences** (A2 hybrid): host‑side shape logic routes\n86\t  T≤threshold to a lightweight kernel; still one immutable candidate.\n87\t  **Hypothesis:** two of five workloads (T=35, 42) are launch/latency bound; cutting launches and\n88\t  host syncs lifts their contribution to the geomean without touching the large‑workload path.\n89\t\n90\tBranching rule: if a candidate regresses geomean or fails correctness, revert to the last accepted\n91\tnode as parent and try the next single change; record the negative result rather than deleting it.\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 3. Per‑candidate execution loop\n96\t\n97\tFor each `cNNN`, in order:\n98\t1. Freeze the intended single change and write it as the source version (one file version).\n99\t2. Run the **static guardrail checklist (§4)** by inspection.\n100\t3. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (consumes 1 evaluation, all five workloads).\n101\t4. Read per‑workload pass/fail + speedup and geomean.\n102\t5. Append one JSON record to `candidates.jsonl` (§6). Never rewrite prior records.\n103\t6. Decide keep / reject / branch per §5; set the parent for the next candidate accordingly.\n104\t\n105\t---\n106\t\n107\t## 4. Correctness checks (static, before every evaluation)\n108\t\n109\t1. **State transpose**: k‑last `[N,H,V,K]` loaded as internal `[K,V]` and stored back transposed.\n110\t2. **GVA indexing**: `q_head = k_head = v_head // 2`, `v_head ∈ [0,8)`; no DRAM expansion of q/k.\n111\t3. **Inclusive causal mask**: output uses post‑update state; intra mask includes the diagonal.\n112\t4. **Gate math in f32**: `x=a.f32()+dt_bias`, `g=exp(-exp(A_log)·softplus(x))`, `beta=sigmoid(b)`;\n113\t   numerically safe `softplus` (`x` large → `x`); cumulative decay in log space (f32).\n114\t5. **Scale** applied to the output only (`scale·q@S`), never to the state update.\n115\t6. **Empty sequences** (`seq_len ≤ 0`): `new_state[seq]=0` (matches reference `continue` before\n116\t   write), no output writes.\n117\t7. **Partial‑chunk masking**: padded tokens contribute 0 to matmuls, UT solve, and state update.\n118\t8. **Dtypes/shapes**: `output` bf16 `[T,8,128]`, `new_state` f32 `[N,8,128,128]`.\n119\t9. **`state is None`** path returns correct zero‑entry‑state behavior.\n120\t10. **Variable‑length iteration** driven by `cu_seqlens`; no reliance on uniform seq length.\n121\t\n122\tIf any check fails on inspection, fix before spending an evaluation.\n123\t\n124\t---\n125\t\n126\t## 5. Decision & stopping criteria\n127\t\n128\t**Per‑candidate decision:**\n129\t- **Reject/invalid** if any workload fails correctness (candidate cannot be selected regardless of\n130\t  speed). Diagnose root cause; branch a fix.\n131\t- **Keep as new baseline** if all five pass and geomean improves over the current best by a\n132\t  meaningful margin (target ≳ 3% geomean, or a clear win on a workload class without regressing\n133\t  others).\n134\t- **Keep‑but‑not‑baseline / record** if all pass but no improvement — log as evidence, revert parent.\n135\t\n136\t**Global stopping (write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with reason) when any holds:**\n137\t- Evaluation budget (100) reached, or token budget approached (stop well before 1.2M hard).\n138\t- Geomean improvement has converged: e.g. ≥3 consecutive accepted‑or‑tried candidates yield <1%\n139\t  geomean gain, and remaining design axes (precision, chunk, block, launch) are exhausted.\n140\t- No valid candidate exists after exhausting the correctness‑fix branches (report failure honestly;\n141\t  never substitute a non‑Triton fallback).\n142\t\n143\t**`final`** is run only after explicit operator approval, on the best valid candidate.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t## 6. Evidence format (`candidates.jsonl`, one JSON object per evaluated candidate)\n148\t\n149\tAppend‑only; never rewrite earlier records. Each record contains:\n150\t\n151\t```json\n152\t{\n153\t  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n154\t  \"parent\": null,\n155\t  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution.py at evaluation time>\",\n156\t  \"phase\": \"A|B|C|D|E\",\n157\t  \"hypothesis\": \"one-sentence claim being tested\",\n158\t  \"change_from_parent\": \"the single variable changed\",\n159\t  \"static_checks\": {\"passed\": true, \"notes\": \"...\"},\n160\t  \"workloads\": [\n161\t    {\"uuid\": \"1efaf2a9...\", \"T\": 42,   \"N\": 2,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n162\t    {\"uuid\": \"d3dc3577...\", \"T\": 35,   \"N\": 1,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n163\t    {\"uuid\": \"1d0cc342...\", \"T\": 294,  \"N\": 3,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n164\t    {\"uuid\": \"15856e8c...\", \"T\": 3028, \"N\": 5,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n165\t    {\"uuid\": \"5d3fc66a...\", \"T\": 8192, \"N\": 34, \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0}\n166\t  ],\n167\t  \"all_correct\": true,\n168\t  \"geomean_speedup\": 0.0,\n169\t  \"decision\": \"keep-baseline|reject|record-only|branch\",\n170\t  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n171\t  \"skills_used\": [],\n172\t  \"notes\": \"diagnosis / next step\"\n173\t}\n174\t```\n175\t\n176\tRules: `all_correct` must be true for a candidate to be selectable; `geomean_speedup` is the primary\n177\tranking metric; `cumulative_evaluations` increments by exactly 1 per record; `skills_used` records\n178\tany KernelWiki/ncu usage (expected empty — see draft §6, Ampere/no‑profiler). Keep hypothesis and\n179\t`change_from_parent` to one clear statement each so the lineage is auditable.\n180\t\n181\t---\n182\t\n183\t## 7. Risk register → mitigation mapping\n184\t\n185\t| Risk (draft §3) | First mitigation | Fallback branch |\n186\t|---|---|---|\n187\t| TF32 drift in `new_state` | c002 uses `ieee`/`tf32x3` on state matmuls | Phase C relaxes only if tolerance holds |\n188\t| UT‑transform conditioning | solve in f32, `C=32` | smaller `C=16` branch |\n189\t| Gate overflow (softplus/exp) | safe softplus, log‑space cumsum in f32 | — |\n190\t| Partial/empty chunks | explicit masking; `new_state=0` for empty seq | dedicated static check #6,#7 |\n191\t| Order‑change error | c001 anchors exact order first | keep c001 as correctness reference |\n192\t| Tiny‑workload overhead | minimize launches/host syncs; Phase E hybrid | route by shape in `run` |\n193\t\n194\t---\n195\t\n196\t## 8. Immediate next actions (subsequent turns, not this one)\n197\t\n198\t1. Implement **c001** (A0 order‑preserving f32 recurrence) per §1 scaffold and §4 checks.\n199\t2. Evaluate c001; record evidence (§6); confirm all five pass and note baseline geomean.\n200\t3. Proceed to **c002** (A1 chunked) only after c001 is green.\n201\t\n202\tNo implementation or evaluation is performed in this turn.\n203\t\n204\t---\n205\t\n206\t## 9. Decision log\n207\t\n208\t- **c001 (Phase A, eval #1) — KEEP-BASELINE.** Order-preserving pure-f32 per-token recurrence.\n209\t  All five workloads PASS (atol=0.01, rtol=0.01, matched_ratio=0.99). Geomean **127.88x**\n210\t  (arith mean 140.06x). Per-workload speedups: 15856e8c=130.9x, 1efaf2a9=107.5x, d3dc3577=69.5x\n211\t  (tiny single-seq, only 16 programs — the geomean floor), 5d3fc66a=255.7x (large, best\n212\t  parallelism), 1d0cc342=136.6x. Correctness is comfortable; the large workload is already fast,\n213\t  and the small/tiny ones are launch/occupancy bound rather than compute bound.\n214\t  **Next (c002):** Phase B chunked gated-delta-rule (UT/WY) to raise throughput on medium/large\n215\t  via tensor cores, keeping state-touching matmuls high-precision, while not regressing the tiny\n216\t  single-seq case. Because the whole op is latency/occupancy-bound (not compute-bound) and c001 is\n217\t  already ~70–256x, the expected upside is moderate; will re-validate correctness and keep the\n218\t  strictest precision that passes.\n219\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fdd07fad-c449-4793-9ccc-9bf69ce8d075","uuid":"4b24a4f8-8c12-445b-9592-a172fb7e00c7","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:58:01.727Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last/docs/plan.md","content":"# Plan — `gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last`\n\nExecutable, sequential KDA optimization plan. Builds on `docs/draft.md`. Target: **A800 `sm_80`\n(Ampere)**, **Triton‑only** compute, submission `solution/solution.py::run(q,k,v,state,A_log,a,\ndt_bias,b,cu_seqlens,scale) -> (output, new_state)`. Budget: **100 evaluations**, tokens 1.0M soft /\n1.2M hard. Evaluate only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`; `final` is\noperator‑only. This turn writes the plan only — **no implementation, no evaluation**.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Guiding principles\n\n1. **Correctness before speed.** The first candidate is the highest‑confidence, order‑preserving\n   f32 implementation. Do not trade precision for speed until a green baseline exists on all five\n   workloads.\n2. **One immutable idea per candidate.** Each `cNNN` is a single, frozen source version encoding one\n   hypothesis. Never mutate an evaluated candidate; never reuse an ID for changed source.\n3. **Spend evaluations deliberately.** Each `feedback` run consumes 1 of 100 evaluations and covers\n   all five workloads. Reason on paper first; only evaluate when the hypothesis is concrete and the\n   guardrail checklist (§4) passes on inspection.\n4. **Change one variable at a time** once past the baseline, so each evaluation attributes a delta to\n   a cause (precision knob, chunk size, block size, launch structure).\n5. **Triton‑only.** No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA‑extension compute fallback. A failing Triton kernel is\n   invalid and must be fixed or abandoned, never silently backed by Torch.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Solution scaffold (fixed across candidates)\n\n`solution/solution.py` structure that stays stable so only kernel/config changes drive new IDs:\n\n- `run(...)`: metadata/launch plumbing only (allowed PyTorch use).\n  - Validate/normalize `scale` (`None`/`0.0 → 1/sqrt(128)`).\n  - Derive `N = cu_seqlens.numel() - 1`, `T`, dtypes; allocate `output [T,8,128] bf16` and\n    `new_state [N,8,128,128] f32` (zero‑initialized so empty sequences are correct by default).\n  - Compute grids on device from `cu_seqlens` **without per‑sequence `.item()` host syncs** where\n    possible (pass `cu_seqlens` into kernels; precompute per‑program `(seq_id, start, len)` mapping\n    on device or with a single cheap host pass).\n  - Handle `state is None` (zero entry state) even though all feedback workloads pass a state.\n  - Launch the Triton kernel(s); return `(output, new_state)`.\n- Triton kernel(s): the actual compute; this is what varies per candidate.\n- Keep any autotune configs and precision constants as explicit compile‑time args so a change to\n  them is a clear new candidate.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Candidate lineage strategy\n\nLineage is a tree rooted at the correctness anchor. Each node names parent, the single change, and\nthe hypothesis. IDs are assigned strictly in evaluation order; a rejected branch does not free its ID.\n\n### Phase A — Correctness anchor\n- **c001 (parent: none) — A0 order‑preserving f32 recurrence.**\n  Grid over `(seq, v_head)` = `N×8`. State `S=[K=128,V=128]` f32 in registers/SMEM. Loop tokens of\n  the sequence performing exact rank‑1 gated delta updates and `o=scale·q@S` on the inclusive state,\n  in f32. GVA `head//2` indexing; V↔K transpose on load/store; empty‑seq `new_state=0`.\n  **Goal:** establish that the pipeline wires up and passes tolerance on all five workloads, and\n  reveal the speedup baseline scale. Expected already ≫1× vs the Python‑loop reference (no host loop,\n  no per‑token launch), but low arithmetic intensity.\n\n### Phase B — Chunked throughput path\n- **c002 (parent: c001) — A1 chunked gated delta rule (UT/WY), conservative & high‑precision.**\n  Chunk `C=32`. Per (seq,head): f32 gate precompute + log‑space cumulative decay; decayed intra\n  matrices; `[C,C]` UT transform solved in f32; sequential inter‑chunk state scan; intra+inter\n  output. State‑touching matmuls use high precision (`input_precision=\"ieee\"` or `\"tf32x3\"`).\n  **Hypothesis:** large speedup on medium/large workloads (15856e8c, 5d3fc66a) while staying within\n  tolerance because summation‑order change is bounded by high‑precision matmuls. If correctness\n  fails → diagnose (mask/transpose/UT) before any perf tuning; if a numerical fail, branch to smaller\n  `C` (c003a) or stricter precision.\n\n### Phase C — Precision tuning (only after B passes)\n- **c00x — precision knob sweep** on state‑touching matmuls: `ieee` → `tf32x3` → default TF32.\n  One candidate per setting. **Hypothesis:** relax toward TF32 for speed as long as `new_state`\n  stays within tolerance. Keep the strictest setting that still passes as the new baseline.\n\n### Phase D — Block/chunk tuning\n- **c00x — chunk size** `C ∈ {16, 32, 64}` (one per candidate).\n- **c00x — V/K blocking** `BV ∈ {64,128}` (occupancy vs tile‑size tradeoff, esp. for 5d3fc66a).\n- **c00x — `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.**\n  **Hypothesis:** 64 KB f32 state tile limits occupancy; smaller `BV` raises program count and helps\n  the large workload; larger tiles help medium. Tune for geomean, re‑validating correctness each step.\n\n### Phase E — Launch structure & overhead (protect the tiny workloads)\n- **c00x — fused vs pipelined** kernels (gate precompute inline vs separate pass).\n- **c00x — low‑overhead path for tiny sequences** (A2 hybrid): host‑side shape logic routes\n  T≤threshold to a lightweight kernel; still one immutable candidate.\n  **Hypothesis:** two of five workloads (T=35, 42) are launch/latency bound; cutting launches and\n  host syncs lifts their contribution to the geomean without touching the large‑workload path.\n\nBranching rule: if a candidate regresses geomean or fails correctness, revert to the last accepted\nnode as parent and try the next single change; record the negative result rather than deleting it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Per‑candidate execution loop\n\nFor each `cNNN`, in order:\n1. Freeze the intended single change and write it as the source version (one file version).\n2. Run the **static guardrail checklist (§4)** by inspection.\n3. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (consumes 1 evaluation, all five workloads).\n4. Read per‑workload pass/fail + speedup and geomean.\n5. Append one JSON record to `candidates.jsonl` (§6). Never rewrite prior records.\n6. Decide keep / reject / branch per §5; set the parent for the next candidate accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Correctness checks (static, before every evaluation)\n\n1. **State transpose**: k‑last `[N,H,V,K]` loaded as internal `[K,V]` and stored back transposed.\n2. **GVA indexing**: `q_head = k_head = v_head // 2`, `v_head ∈ [0,8)`; no DRAM expansion of q/k.\n3. **Inclusive causal mask**: output uses post‑update state; intra mask includes the diagonal.\n4. **Gate math in f32**: `x=a.f32()+dt_bias`, `g=exp(-exp(A_log)·softplus(x))`, `beta=sigmoid(b)`;\n   numerically safe `softplus` (`x` large → `x`); cumulative decay in log space (f32).\n5. **Scale** applied to the output only (`scale·q@S`), never to the state update.\n6. **Empty sequences** (`seq_len ≤ 0`): `new_state[seq]=0` (matches reference `continue` before\n   write), no output writes.\n7. **Partial‑chunk masking**: padded tokens contribute 0 to matmuls, UT solve, and state update.\n8. **Dtypes/shapes**: `output` bf16 `[T,8,128]`, `new_state` f32 `[N,8,128,128]`.\n9. **`state is None`** path returns correct zero‑entry‑state behavior.\n10. **Variable‑length iteration** driven by `cu_seqlens`; no reliance on uniform seq length.\n\nIf any check fails on inspection, fix before spending an evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decision & stopping criteria\n\n**Per‑candidate decision:**\n- **Reject/invalid** if any workload fails correctness (candidate cannot be selected regardless of\n  speed). Diagnose root cause; branch a fix.\n- **Keep as new baseline** if all five pass and geomean improves over the current best by a\n  meaningful margin (target ≳ 3% geomean, or a clear win on a workload class without regressing\n  others).\n- **Keep‑but‑not‑baseline / record** if all pass but no improvement — log as evidence, revert parent.\n\n**Global stopping (write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with reason) when any holds:**\n- Evaluation budget (100) reached, or token budget approached (stop well before 1.2M hard).\n- Geomean improvement has converged: e.g. ≥3 consecutive accepted‑or‑tried candidates yield <1%\n  geomean gain, and remaining design axes (precision, chunk, block, launch) are exhausted.\n- No valid candidate exists after exhausting the correctness‑fix branches (report failure honestly;\n  never substitute a non‑Triton fallback).\n\n**`final`** is run only after explicit operator approval, on the best valid candidate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Evidence format (`candidates.jsonl`, one JSON object per evaluated candidate)\n\nAppend‑only; never rewrite earlier records. Each record contains:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution.py at evaluation time>\",\n  \"phase\": \"A|B|C|D|E\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"one-sentence claim being tested\",\n  \"change_from_parent\": \"the single variable changed\",\n  \"static_checks\": {\"passed\": true, \"notes\": \"...\"},\n  \"workloads\": [\n    {\"uuid\": \"1efaf2a9...\", \"T\": 42,   \"N\": 2,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n    {\"uuid\": \"d3dc3577...\", \"T\": 35,   \"N\": 1,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n    {\"uuid\": \"1d0cc342...\", \"T\": 294,  \"N\": 3,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n    {\"uuid\": \"15856e8c...\", \"T\": 3028, \"N\": 5,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0},\n    {\"uuid\": \"5d3fc66a...\", \"T\": 8192, \"N\": 34, \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.0}\n  ],\n  \"all_correct\": true,\n  \"geomean_speedup\": 0.0,\n  \"decision\": \"keep-baseline|reject|record-only|branch\",\n  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n  \"skills_used\": [],\n  \"notes\": \"diagnosis / next step\"\n}\n```\n\nRules: `all_correct` must be true for a candidate to be selectable; `geomean_speedup` is the primary\nranking metric; `cumulative_evaluations` increments by exactly 1 per record; `skills_used` records\nany KernelWiki/ncu usage (expected empty — see draft §6, Ampere/no‑profiler). Keep hypothesis and\n`change_from_parent` to one clear statement each so the lineage is auditable.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Risk register → mitigation mapping\n\n| Risk (draft §3) | First mitigation | Fallback branch |\n|---|---|---|\n| TF32 drift in `new_state` | c002 uses `ieee`/`tf32x3` on state matmuls | Phase C relaxes only if tolerance holds |\n| UT‑transform conditioning | solve in f32, `C=32` | smaller `C=16` branch |\n| Gate overflow (softplus/exp) | safe softplus, log‑space cumsum in f32 | — |\n| Partial/empty chunks | explicit masking; `new_state=0` for empty seq | dedicated static check #6,#7 |\n| Order‑change error | c001 anchors exact order first | keep c001 as correctness reference |\n| Tiny‑workload overhead | minimize launches/host syncs; Phase E hybrid | route by shape in `run` |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Immediate next actions (subsequent turns, not this one)\n\n1. Implement **c001** (A0 order‑preserving f32 recurrence) per §1 scaffold and §4 checks.\n2. Evaluate c001; record evidence (§6); confirm all five pass and note baseline geomean.\n3. Proceed to **c002** (A1 chunked) only after c001 is green.\n\nNo implementation or evaluation is performed in this turn.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Decision log\n\n- **c001 (Phase A, eval #1) — KEEP-BASELINE.** Order-preserving pure-f32 per-token recurrence.\n  All five workloads PASS (atol=0.01, rtol=0.01, matched_ratio=0.99). Geomean **127.88x**\n  (arith mean 140.06x). Per-workload speedups: 15856e8c=130.9x, 1efaf2a9=107.5x, d3dc3577=69.5x\n  (tiny single-seq, only 16 programs — the geomean floor), 5d3fc66a=255.7x (large, best\n  parallelism), 1d0cc342=136.6x. Correctness is comfortable; the large workload is already fast,\n  and the small/tiny ones are launch/occupancy bound rather than compute bound.\n  **Next (c002):** Phase B chunked gated-delta-rule (UT/WY) to raise throughput on medium/large\n  via tensor cores, keeping state-touching matmuls high-precision, while not regressing the tiny\n  single-seq case. Because the whole op is latency/occupancy-bound (not compute-bound) and c001 is\n  already ~70–256x, the expected upside is moderate; will re-validate correctness and keep the\n  strictest precision that passes.\n","numLines":219,"startLine":1,"totalLines":219}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y23wMNo7CDfSGwEy4dpYi9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — `gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last`\n2\t\n3\tGated Delta Net (GDN) prefill, GVA head configuration, **k‑last** state layout, captured from\n4\tQwen3‑Next linear‑attention layers (TP=4). Target hardware: **NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**.\n5\tPrimary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing.\n6\t\n7\tThis draft analyzes the operation, constraints, numerical risks, the Triton design space, and the\n8\tvalidation strategy. No plan and no code are produced in this step.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. Operation analysis\n13\t\n14\t### 1.1 Shapes, dtypes, and constants\n15\t\n16\tConstant axes (from `task/definition.json`):\n17\t\n18\t- `num_q_heads (Hq) = 4`, `num_k_heads (Hk) = 4`, `num_v_heads (Hv) = 8`, `head_size (D) = 128`.\n19\t- `num_sab_heads = max(Hq, Hv) = 8` — outputs and state are laid out over the **8 value heads**.\n20\t- GVA expansion factor `Hv // Hq = 2` (each q/k head is shared by two v heads).\n21\t\n22\tVariable axes: `total_seq_len (T)`, `num_seqs (N)`, `len_cu_seqlens = N + 1`.\n23\t\n24\tInputs:\n25\t\n26\t| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n27\t|---|---|---|---|\n28\t| `q` | `[T, 4, 128]` | bf16 | query, 4 heads |\n29\t| `k` | `[T, 4, 128]` | bf16 | key, 4 heads |\n30\t| `v` | `[T, 8, 128]` | bf16 | value, 8 heads |\n31\t| `state` | `[N, 8, 128, 128]` | f32 | recurrent state, **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`**, optional |\n32\t| `A_log` | `[8]` | f32 | log decay parameter (per v‑head) |\n33\t| `a` | `[T, 8]` | bf16 | input‑dependent decay (per v‑head) |\n34\t| `dt_bias` | `[8]` | f32 | decay bias (per v‑head) |\n35\t| `b` | `[T, 8]` | bf16 | update‑gate input (per v‑head) |\n36\t| `cu_seqlens` | `[N+1]` | int64 | variable‑length batching offsets |\n37\t| `scale` | scalar f32 | | fixed `0.08838834764831843 = 1/sqrt(128)` in all feedback workloads |\n38\t\n39\tOutputs:\n40\t\n41\t| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n42\t|---|---|---|---|\n43\t| `output` | `[T, 8, 128]` | bf16 | attention output over the 8 v‑heads |\n44\t| `new_state` | `[N, 8, 128, 128]` | f32 | updated recurrent state, **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`** |\n45\t\n46\tConstraints: `len_cu_seqlens == N + 1`, `T == cu_seqlens[-1]`. Sequences are contiguous slices of the\n47\ttoken axis given by `cu_seqlens`.\n48\t\n49\t### 1.2 Gate / gating math (per token `t`, per v‑head `h`)\n50\t\n51\t```\n52\tx    = a.float() + dt_bias.float()                      # [T, 8]\n53\tg    = exp( -exp(A_log.float()) * softplus(x) )          # [T, 8]  decay in (0, 1]\n54\tbeta = sigmoid(b.float())                                # [T, 8]  in (0, 1)\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t`softplus(x) = log(1 + exp(x)) ≥ 0`, `exp(A_log) > 0`, so the exponent is `≤ 0` and `g ∈ (0, 1]`.\n58\t`g` is a **scalar** per (token, head), so it scales the whole `[K,V]` state uniformly (not a\n59\tper‑channel diagonal). This matters: the cumulative within‑chunk decay is a scalar cumulative product.\n60\t\n61\t### 1.3 The recurrence (reference semantics)\n62\t\n63\tInternally the reference transposes the k‑last input state `[H, V, K]` → `state_HKV = [H, K, V]`\n64\t(K first, V last). For each token `t` in a sequence, with `q_t, k_t ∈ R^K` (K=128), `v_t ∈ R^V`\n65\t(V=128), scalars `g_t, beta_t`:\n66\t\n67\t```\n68\told   = g_t * S_{t-1}                 # [K, V]   apply decay\n69\told_v = k_tᵀ @ old                    # [V]      = k_t · columns of old\n70\tnew_v = beta_t * v_t + (1-beta_t) * old_v\n71\tS_t   = old + k_t ⊗ (new_v - old_v)   # [K, V]   rank‑1 update\n72\t      = old + beta_t * k_t ⊗ (v_t - old_v)          (algebraically identical)\n73\to_t   = scale * q_tᵀ @ S_t            # [V]      output uses the UPDATED (inclusive) state\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\tKey observations:\n77\t\n78\t- This is the **gated delta rule**: `S_t = g_t S_{t-1} + k_t ⊗ u_t` with pseudo‑value\n79\t  `u_t = beta_t (v_t − k_tᵀ (g_t S_{t-1}))`.\n80\t- The output at token `t` uses `S_t` (**inclusive** of the current token) — the intra‑chunk\n81\t  attention mask is lower‑triangular **including the diagonal**.\n82\t- GVA: for v‑head `h ∈ [0,8)`, `q` and `k` come from head `h // 2` (this reproduces\n83\t  `repeat_interleave(2)` without materializing an expanded tensor).\n84\t- `q`, `k`, `v` are bf16 in memory; `state` is f32. The reference performs all matmuls in **float32**\n85\t  (`a.float() @ b.float()`), and the state accumulates in f32.\n86\t- Final store: `new_state[seq] = S_last.transpose(-1,-2)` → back to k‑last `[H, V, K]`.\n87\t\n88\t### 1.4 Chunk‑parallel reformulation (target for high performance)\n89\t\n90\tThe reference is an O(T) Python loop over tokens — catastrophically slow. The standard fast form is\n91\tthe **chunked gated delta rule** (WY / UT‑transform), which is what\n92\t`flashinfer.gdn.chunk_gated_delta_rule` implements. Split each sequence into chunks of size `C`\n93\t(e.g. 32/64). Within a chunk with entry state `S₀`, define the scalar cumulative decay\n94\t`γ_i = Π_{j≤i} g_j` (γ₀ across the chunk boundary handled in log space):\n95\t\n96\t```\n97\tS_i = γ_i S₀ + Σ_{l≤i} (γ_i/γ_l) k_l ⊗ u_l\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\tPseudo‑value `u_l = beta_l (v_l − k_lᵀ (g_l S_{l-1}))` depends on earlier `u_m (m<l)`, giving a\n101\tlower‑triangular linear system solved once per chunk (the **UT / WY transform**, i.e. inverting\n102\t`(I + tril(M,-1))` where `M_{lm} = beta_l (γ_l/γ_m)(k_l·k_m)`). Building blocks per chunk:\n103\t\n104\t1. **Preprocess**: `log g`, `beta`, within‑chunk cumulative `Γ = cumsum(log g)` (log space, f32).\n105\t2. **Intra matrices**: decayed `A = tril((q k^T) or (k k^T) weighted by γ_i/γ_l)`.\n106\t3. **UT transform**: solve the strictly‑lower‑triangular system to get `U` (and `W` for the state\n107\t   contribution). Sizes are `[C, C]` and `[C, V]`, `[C, K]`.\n108\t4. **Inter‑chunk scan** (sequential over chunks per (seq,head)): `S_new = γ_C S₀ + (K_scaled)ᵀ U`.\n109\t5. **Output**: `o = scale * ( intra: tril‑masked decayed (q k^T) @ U  +  inter: γ ⊙ (q @ S₀) )`.\n110\t\n111\tParallelism: independent across `N × Hv` (= up to `34×8 = 272` programs for the largest workload)\n112\tand across chunks *within* the intra‑chunk matmuls; the inter‑chunk scan is sequential per\n113\t(seq, head). Compute is tiny (≈100k FLOP/token/head ⇒ ≈6.5 GFLOP at T=8192) — the op is\n114\t**latency/dependency‑bound, not compute‑bound**, so kernel‑launch count and occupancy matter more\n115\tthan raw MMA throughput.\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## 2. Constraints (hard rules that shape the design)\n120\t\n121\tFrom `CLAUDE.md` / `TASK.md`:\n122\t\n123\t- **Triton‑only compute.** No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA‑extension/alternate‑impl fallback. A failing\n124\t  Triton kernel is *invalid* — it may not be silently backed by a Torch path. PyTorch usage is\n125\t  limited to tensor metadata, allocation, and launch plumbing (e.g. computing grids, `cu_seqlens`\n126\t  handling, output allocation).\n127\t- **Submission surface**: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(q, k, v, state, A_log, a, dt_bias, b,\n128\t  cu_seqlens, scale)` returning `(output, new_state)` with the exact shapes/dtypes above.\n129\t- **Immutable candidates.** Implement `c001`, `c002`, … sequentially, one source version at a time;\n130\t  never reuse an ID for changed source; append one JSON record per evaluation to `candidates.jsonl`\n131\t  without rewriting prior records.\n132\t- **Evaluation.** Only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five fixed feedback\n133\t  workloads together count as one evaluation. Budget: **100 evaluations**. Token soft/hard limits:\n134\t  1.0M / 1.2M. `final` is operator‑only.\n135\t- **Isolation.** Work only in this workspace; do not inspect parent dirs, baselines, evaluator,\n136\t  controller, or other tasks. Permitted external knowledge sources are the `KernelWiki` and\n137\t  `ncu-report-skill` skills only — but see §6 on their applicability here. No web/subagents/MCP.\n138\t- **Do not run** CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, the external evaluator directly, or any alternate\n139\t  correctness harness. (In this environment the general `Bash` tool is also disabled, so the *only*\n140\t  sanctioned execution channel is the `evaluate_candidate.sh` launcher.)\n141\t- **Metric.** Geometric‑mean speedup across selected workloads; **every** workload must pass\n142\t  correctness or the candidate is invalid.\n143\t\n144\tLayout / semantics constraints to respect exactly:\n145\t\n146\t- State I/O is **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`**; internally the recurrence is on `[K, V]`. Load with a\n147\t  V↔K transpose relative to storage; store new_state transposed back. Getting the V/K axes swapped\n148\t  is the single most likely functional bug — call it out in validation.\n149\t- Output is over **8 v‑heads**; `q`/`k` are indexed with `head // 2` (GVA), never expanded in DRAM\n150\t  unless deliberately chosen.\n151\t- Output uses the **inclusive** (post‑update) state ⇒ diagonal‑inclusive causal mask.\n152\t- `scale` multiplies only the final output (`q @ S_t`), not the state update.\n153\t- Variable‑length batching: iterate sequences via `cu_seqlens`; sequences may be as short as a few\n154\t  tokens (workloads have seqs down to ~10–35 tokens) and empty sequences (`seq_len ≤ 0`) must be\n155\t  skipped (contribute nothing, but `new_state` still written — check reference: empty seq leaves\n156\t  `new_state[seq]` as the zero‑init from `torch.zeros`, since the inner loop never runs and the\n157\t  `state_HKV`... actually it `continue`s before writing → `new_state[seq]` stays **zeros**, not the\n158\t  input state. Must replicate: for `seq_len ≤ 0`, `new_state[seq] = 0`, not the passed‑in state).\n159\t\n160\t### 2.1 Feedback workload profile\n161\t\n162\t| uuid (short) | T | N | avg tok/seq | regime |\n163\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n164\t| 1efaf2a9 | 42 | 2 | 21 | tiny — launch/latency bound |\n165\t| d3dc3577 | 35 | 1 | 35 | tiny, single seq (only 8 programs) |\n166\t| 1d0cc342 | 294 | 3 | 98 | small |\n167\t| 15856e8c | 3028 | 5 | 606 | medium |\n168\t| 5d3fc66a | 8192 | 34 | 241 | large (best parallelism: 272 seq·head programs) |\n169\t\n170\tImplications:\n171\t- Two of five workloads are *tiny* (T=35, 42). There, fixed overheads (kernel launches, gate\n172\t  precompute, `.item()` host syncs on `cu_seqlens`) dominate. Minimizing launch count and avoiding\n173\t  host↔device syncs is as important as the main kernel. Prefer passing `cu_seqlens` to the kernel and\n174\t  computing grids without per‑sequence `.item()` loops on the host.\n175\t- The large workload rewards the chunked/MMA path and good occupancy; small ones reward low overhead.\n176\t- A single immutable kernel must handle all five ⇒ favor a design whose chunk size / block sizes are\n177\t  robust across `seq_len` from ~10 to ~600 (mask/pad partial chunks correctly).\n178\t\n179\t---\n180\t\n181\t## 3. Numerical risks\n182\t\n183\tThe reference computes **everything in float32** (bf16 operands cast up, f32 matmuls, f32 state).\n184\tOutput is bf16, `new_state` is f32. Both are checked. Risks, ordered by importance:\n185\t\n186\t1. **State matmul precision (TF32 vs f32).** On Ampere, `tl.dot` with f32 operands defaults to TF32\n187\t   (~10‑bit mantissa). The matmuls that touch the f32 state (`k @ old_state`, `q @ S₀`, and the\n188\t   `Kᵀ @ U` state update) would then round f32 operands to TF32. Over up to `T/C ≈ 128` sequential\n189\t   chunks the error in `new_state` (a checked f32 output) can accumulate. Mitigations to keep in the\n190\t   toolbox: use `tl.dot(..., input_precision=\"ieee\")` (true f32, slower) or `\"tf32x3\"` (3‑pass, near\n191\t   f32) for state‑touching matmuls; keep the state accumulator itself in f32 registers/SMEM and add\n192\t   (not MMA) rank‑1 updates in the naive path. The bf16→f32 output has ~8‑bit mantissa, so TF32 is\n193\t   *likely* fine for `output`; `new_state` is the tighter target.\n194\t\n195\t2. **bf16 operand matmuls are essentially exact vs reference.** `q,k,v` are already bf16 in DRAM, so\n196\t   a bf16×bf16→f32 MMA computes the same products as the reference's f32 matmul of bf16‑origin\n197\t   operands, only differing in accumulation order. So `q k^T`, `k k^T` intra‑chunk matmuls carry\n198\t   negligible extra error — the precision worry is specifically the *f32* state operands (point 1).\n199\t\n200\t3. **Gate stability / log‑space accumulation.** Compute `log g = -exp(A_log)*softplus(x)` and do the\n201\t   within‑chunk cumulative decay as a **cumsum in log space (f32)**, exponentiating ratios\n202\t   `exp(Γ_i − Γ_l)` (always `≤ 0` exponent ⇒ `≤ 1`, no overflow; underflow to 0 is the physically\n203\t   correct vanishing of old contributions). Use a numerically safe `softplus` (`log1p(exp(x))` with\n204\t   the `x` large branch → `x`) to avoid `exp` overflow for large `x`.\n205\t\n206\t4. **UT transform conditioning.** The `(I + tril(M,-1))⁻¹` solve within a chunk is well‑conditioned\n207\t   for reasonable `beta` and decay (strictly lower‑triangular nilpotent ⇒ the inverse is a finite\n208\t   Neumann series of length `C`), but large `beta·(k·k)` products with slow decay can amplify error.\n209\t   Solve in f32. Smaller chunk `C` (e.g. 32) reduces conditioning risk at some throughput cost.\n210\t\n211\t5. **Sigmoid/softplus in bf16 inputs.** `a`, `b` are bf16 — cast to f32 *before* `+dt_bias`,\n212\t   `softplus`, `sigmoid` (reference does `a.float()`, `b.float()`). Doing gate math in bf16 would\n213\t   diverge.\n214\t\n215\t6. **Empty / partial chunks & masking.** Partial last chunks (seq_len not divisible by `C`) must be\n216\t   masked so out‑of‑range tokens contribute 0 to matmuls, the UT solve, and the state update.\n217\t   Off‑by‑one in the inclusive‑diagonal mask flips the semantics.\n218\t\n219\t7. **Accumulation order across chunks.** The reference is a pure left‑to‑right scalar recurrence.\n220\t   Chunking changes summation order; combined with TF32 this is the main source of `new_state`\n221\t   drift. The **naive per‑token Triton path reproduces the exact recurrence order** and is the safest\n222\t   correctness anchor.\n223\t\n224\t8. **Tolerances are not specified** in `definition.json`. Treat them as unknown; the official\n225\t   evaluator is the source of truth (pass/fail per workload). Because I cannot run a local harness,\n226\t   the first candidate must be the highest‑confidence (order‑preserving, f32) implementation to\n227\t   establish that correctness passes at all, before trading precision for speed.\n228\t\n229\t---\n230\t\n231\t## 4. Triton design space\n232\t\n233\tPrimitives available on `sm_80`: bf16/f16 tensor cores (bf16×bf16→f32), TF32 for f32 MMA, up to\n234\t~164 KB shared memory per SM (opt‑in), `tl.dot`, `tl.cumsum`, `tl.associative_scan`,\n235\t`input_precision` control. No TMA/tcgen05/warp‑spec (those are Hopper/Blackwell).\n236\t\n237\tState footprint per (seq, head): `[K=128, V=128]` f32 = **64 KB**. This is the central resource\n238\tconstraint: it fits in registers distributed across a warp‑group accumulator, or in SMEM, but limits\n239\toccupancy. Splitting `V` (or `K`) into blocks (e.g. `BV=64`) halves the tile to 32 KB and raises the\n240\tprogram count — a key tuning axis.\n241\t\n242\t### Axis A — algorithmic strategy\n243\t\n244\t- **A0. Naive per‑token recurrence (correctness anchor / first candidate).**\n245\t  One program per `(seq, v_head)` (grid `N×8`). Keep `S = [128,128]` f32 in registers/SMEM; loop\n246\t  over the sequence's tokens performing exact rank‑1 updates and the `q@S` output with f32 (no\n247\t  tensor cores, or bf16 MMA only where exact). Reproduces the reference's order → highest correctness\n248\t  confidence. Far faster than the Python reference (no host loop, no per‑token kernel launch), but\n249\t  low arithmetic intensity and serial per head. Good baseline to *pass first*, then beat.\n250\t\n251\t- **A1. Chunked gated delta rule (UT/WY transform) — main performance target.**\n252\t  Chunk size `C ∈ {16,32,64}`. Per (seq,head): preprocess gates, build decayed intra matrices, solve\n253\t  the `[C,C]` UT transform, sequential inter‑chunk state scan, then intra+inter output. Uses tensor\n254\t  cores for the `[C,·]` matmuls. Best throughput on the medium/large workloads. Higher\n255\t  implementation and numerical risk (§3.1, §3.4). Typically several fused sub‑kernels.\n256\t\n257\t- **A2. Hybrid.** Naive per‑token for tiny sequences (T≤ threshold), chunked for the rest — but a\n258\t  *single immutable kernel/config* must serve all five workloads, so any branch must be inside the\n259\t  kernel or chosen by host‑side shape logic that is still one candidate. Keep as a later refinement.\n260\t\n261\t### Axis B — parallelization & tiling\n262\t\n263\t- Grid over `(seq, head)`; optionally add a `V`‑block (`BV`) axis for occupancy. The inter‑chunk scan\n264\t  is sequential per (seq,head,V‑block); the `K` reduction is internal.\n265\t- `BK`, `BV` block sizes (64 or 128); `C` chunk size; `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`; `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.\n266\t- For tiny workloads, favor fewer/larger programs to cut launch overhead; for the large workload,\n267\t  favor more programs (smaller `BV`) for occupancy. This tension is the main autotune target.\n268\t\n269\t### Axis C — precision knobs\n270\t\n271\t- `input_precision`/`allow_tf32` per matmul: bf16 operands → default; f32/state operands →\n272\t  `\"ieee\"` or `\"tf32x3\"` (§3.1). Expose as a compile‑time constant to A/B against the evaluator.\n273\t- Keep gate math and the log‑cumulative decay strictly in f32.\n274\t\n275\t### Axis D — layout & memory\n276\t\n277\t- Load `state` `[V,K]`→ transpose to `[K,V]` on load; store `new_state` transposed back. Use masked\n278\t  loads for partial chunks and to zero empty sequences' `new_state`.\n279\t- Avoid materializing GVA‑expanded `q_exp`/`k_exp` in DRAM: index `head//2` inside the kernel.\n280\t- Compute `g`/`beta` either in a small fused preprocessing kernel over `[T,8]` or inline per chunk.\n281\t  Inline avoids an extra pass but recomputes; a tiny separate pass is cheap and simplifies the main\n282\t  kernel — decide by measurement.\n283\t- Minimize host↔device syncs: pass `cu_seqlens` to the kernel and derive per‑program `(start, len)`\n284\t  on device; avoid `.item()` per sequence (hurts the tiny workloads most).\n285\t\n286\t### Axis E — launch structure\n287\t\n288\t- Single fused main kernel vs. a small pipeline (gate precompute → chunk prep → scan → output).\n289\t  Fewer launches help tiny workloads; a pipeline can raise throughput on the large one. Start fused\n290\t  and simple; split only if profiling‑by‑evaluation (indirect) suggests it.\n291\t\n292\t### Candidate ladder (sequencing intent, not a plan)\n293\t\n294\t1. `c001` = A0 (order‑preserving f32 recurrence) — establish correctness passes on all five.\n295\t2. Introduce chunking / tensor cores (A1) with conservative `C` and high‑precision state matmuls;\n296\t   confirm correctness still passes, measure speedup.\n297\t3. Tune precision knobs (Axis C), block sizes and chunk size (Axis B), and launch structure (Axis E)\n298\t   for geomean, re‑validating correctness at each immutable step.\n299\t4. Optionally hybridize (A2) for the tiny workloads if they dominate the geomean.\n300\t\n301\t---\n302\t\n303\t## 5. Validation strategy\n304\t\n305\t- **Source of truth = official evaluator only.** I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, or any local\n306\t  correctness harness (both by rule and because `Bash` is disabled here). Each\n307\t  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` runs all five workloads and consumes one of the\n308\t  100 evaluations. Therefore: reason precisely on paper first; do not spend evaluations on\n309\t  speculative variants.\n310\t- **Correctness‑first ordering.** `c001` is deliberately the order‑preserving f32 recurrence so the\n311\t  first evaluation answers \"does the pipeline wire up and pass tolerance at all?\" before any\n312\t  precision/perf trade. Only after a green baseline do I introduce chunking and tensor cores.\n313\t- **Guardrails I will self‑check before every evaluation** (cheap, catches the likely bugs):\n314\t  1. State V↔K transpose on both load and store (k‑last `[N,H,V,K]` ⇄ internal `[K,V]`).\n315\t  2. GVA indexing `q_head = k_head = h // 2` for `h ∈ [0,8)`.\n316\t  3. Inclusive (diagonal) causal mask; output uses post‑update state; `scale` on output only.\n317\t  4. Gate math in f32: `x=a.f32()+dt_bias`, `g=exp(-exp(A_log)*softplus(x))`, `beta=sigmoid(b)`.\n318\t  5. Empty sequences (`seq_len ≤ 0`) ⇒ `new_state[seq] = 0` (matches reference `continue` before\n319\t     writing), `output` untouched (zeros) for those tokens (there are none).\n320\t  6. Output dtype bf16, `new_state` dtype f32; exact output/new_state shapes.\n321\t  7. Partial‑chunk masking so padding contributes 0 to matmuls / UT solve / state update.\n322\t  8. Handle `state is None` (all feedback workloads *do* pass a state, but keep the signature honest).\n323\t- **Metric interpretation.** Rank by geometric‑mean speedup across the five workloads; a candidate is\n324\t  only valid if *all five* pass. Because two workloads are tiny and two are medium/large, the geomean\n325\t  balances launch overhead vs. throughput — I will watch that a throughput win on the large workload\n326\t  doesn't regress the tiny ones (and vice versa).\n327\t- **Evidence recording.** For every evaluated candidate append one complete JSON record to\n328\t  `candidates.jsonl` with: parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation status, per‑workload result\n329\t  (pass/fail + speedup), geomean, decision (keep/reject/branch), cumulative evaluation count, and\n330\t  skill usage. Never rewrite earlier records.\n331\t- **Convergence / stop.** Stop at the evaluation or token budget, or when successive immutable\n332\t  candidates no longer improve geomean meaningfully; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n333\t  Never run `final` without explicit operator approval.\n334\t\n335\t---\n336\t\n337\t## 6. Skill applicability note\n338\t\n339\t- **KernelWiki** targets NVIDIA **Blackwell (SM100/B200)** and **Hopper (SM90/H100)** techniques\n340\t  (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/NVFP4/2‑SM, warp specialization, TMA, FA‑4, etc.). This task runs on **A800\n341\t  (`sm_80`, Ampere)**, which has none of those features, so KernelWiki is out of scope and I do not\n342\t  expect to invoke it; the relevant primitives here are Ampere bf16/TF32 tensor cores, SMEM, and\n343\t  Triton scans. If a genuinely Hopper/Blackwell‑specific question arises it would not apply to this\n344\t  hardware anyway.\n345\t- **ncu-report-skill** requires running Nsight Compute on a B200 (`sm_100`) and profiling, which is\n346\t  both hardware‑mismatched and forbidden here (\"do not run a profiler\"). I will therefore not use it;\n347\t  performance signal comes indirectly from the official evaluator's measured speedups.\n348\t\n349\t---\n350\t\n351\t## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via the evaluator, minimizing spend)\n352\t\n353\t1. Exact correctness tolerance — inferred from whether `c001` (f32, order‑preserving) passes.\n354\t2. What baseline the speedup is measured against (naive Python reference vs. an optimized kernel).\n355\t   The Python‑loop reference is extremely slow, so even A0 should show large speedups; the geomean\n356\t   scale will reveal the baseline's nature.\n357\t3. Whether TF32 state matmuls stay within `new_state` tolerance, or `\"ieee\"`/`\"tf32x3\"` is required.\n358\t4. Best `C`/`BV`/`num_warps`/`num_stages` per the workload mix, and whether the tiny workloads need a\n359\t   low‑overhead path (A2) to protect the geomean.\n360\t\n361\t**Next step:** write `docs/plan.md` (executable plan), then implement `c001` as the order‑preserving\n362\tf32 correctness anchor. Not done in this turn.\n363\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fdd07fad-c449-4793-9ccc-9bf69ce8d075","uuid":"539393e9-6537-4228-b084-9da98190b990","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:58:01.729Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — `gdn_prefill_qk4_v8_d128_k_last`\n\nGated Delta Net (GDN) prefill, GVA head configuration, **k‑last** state layout, captured from\nQwen3‑Next linear‑attention layers (TP=4). Target hardware: **NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**.\nPrimary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing.\n\nThis draft analyzes the operation, constraints, numerical risks, the Triton design space, and the\nvalidation strategy. No plan and no code are produced in this step.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation analysis\n\n### 1.1 Shapes, dtypes, and constants\n\nConstant axes (from `task/definition.json`):\n\n- `num_q_heads (Hq) = 4`, `num_k_heads (Hk) = 4`, `num_v_heads (Hv) = 8`, `head_size (D) = 128`.\n- `num_sab_heads = max(Hq, Hv) = 8` — outputs and state are laid out over the **8 value heads**.\n- GVA expansion factor `Hv // Hq = 2` (each q/k head is shared by two v heads).\n\nVariable axes: `total_seq_len (T)`, `num_seqs (N)`, `len_cu_seqlens = N + 1`.\n\nInputs:\n\n| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `q` | `[T, 4, 128]` | bf16 | query, 4 heads |\n| `k` | `[T, 4, 128]` | bf16 | key, 4 heads |\n| `v` | `[T, 8, 128]` | bf16 | value, 8 heads |\n| `state` | `[N, 8, 128, 128]` | f32 | recurrent state, **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`**, optional |\n| `A_log` | `[8]` | f32 | log decay parameter (per v‑head) |\n| `a` | `[T, 8]` | bf16 | input‑dependent decay (per v‑head) |\n| `dt_bias` | `[8]` | f32 | decay bias (per v‑head) |\n| `b` | `[T, 8]` | bf16 | update‑gate input (per v‑head) |\n| `cu_seqlens` | `[N+1]` | int64 | variable‑length batching offsets |\n| `scale` | scalar f32 | | fixed `0.08838834764831843 = 1/sqrt(128)` in all feedback workloads |\n\nOutputs:\n\n| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `output` | `[T, 8, 128]` | bf16 | attention output over the 8 v‑heads |\n| `new_state` | `[N, 8, 128, 128]` | f32 | updated recurrent state, **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`** |\n\nConstraints: `len_cu_seqlens == N + 1`, `T == cu_seqlens[-1]`. Sequences are contiguous slices of the\ntoken axis given by `cu_seqlens`.\n\n### 1.2 Gate / gating math (per token `t`, per v‑head `h`)\n\n```\nx    = a.float() + dt_bias.float()                      # [T, 8]\ng    = exp( -exp(A_log.float()) * softplus(x) )          # [T, 8]  decay in (0, 1]\nbeta = sigmoid(b.float())                                # [T, 8]  in (0, 1)\n```\n\n`softplus(x) = log(1 + exp(x)) ≥ 0`, `exp(A_log) > 0`, so the exponent is `≤ 0` and `g ∈ (0, 1]`.\n`g` is a **scalar** per (token, head), so it scales the whole `[K,V]` state uniformly (not a\nper‑channel diagonal). This matters: the cumulative within‑chunk decay is a scalar cumulative product.\n\n### 1.3 The recurrence (reference semantics)\n\nInternally the reference transposes the k‑last input state `[H, V, K]` → `state_HKV = [H, K, V]`\n(K first, V last). For each token `t` in a sequence, with `q_t, k_t ∈ R^K` (K=128), `v_t ∈ R^V`\n(V=128), scalars `g_t, beta_t`:\n\n```\nold   = g_t * S_{t-1}                 # [K, V]   apply decay\nold_v = k_tᵀ @ old                    # [V]      = k_t · columns of old\nnew_v = beta_t * v_t + (1-beta_t) * old_v\nS_t   = old + k_t ⊗ (new_v - old_v)   # [K, V]   rank‑1 update\n      = old + beta_t * k_t ⊗ (v_t - old_v)          (algebraically identical)\no_t   = scale * q_tᵀ @ S_t            # [V]      output uses the UPDATED (inclusive) state\n```\n\nKey observations:\n\n- This is the **gated delta rule**: `S_t = g_t S_{t-1} + k_t ⊗ u_t` with pseudo‑value\n  `u_t = beta_t (v_t − k_tᵀ (g_t S_{t-1}))`.\n- The output at token `t` uses `S_t` (**inclusive** of the current token) — the intra‑chunk\n  attention mask is lower‑triangular **including the diagonal**.\n- GVA: for v‑head `h ∈ [0,8)`, `q` and `k` come from head `h // 2` (this reproduces\n  `repeat_interleave(2)` without materializing an expanded tensor).\n- `q`, `k`, `v` are bf16 in memory; `state` is f32. The reference performs all matmuls in **float32**\n  (`a.float() @ b.float()`), and the state accumulates in f32.\n- Final store: `new_state[seq] = S_last.transpose(-1,-2)` → back to k‑last `[H, V, K]`.\n\n### 1.4 Chunk‑parallel reformulation (target for high performance)\n\nThe reference is an O(T) Python loop over tokens — catastrophically slow. The standard fast form is\nthe **chunked gated delta rule** (WY / UT‑transform), which is what\n`flashinfer.gdn.chunk_gated_delta_rule` implements. Split each sequence into chunks of size `C`\n(e.g. 32/64). Within a chunk with entry state `S₀`, define the scalar cumulative decay\n`γ_i = Π_{j≤i} g_j` (γ₀ across the chunk boundary handled in log space):\n\n```\nS_i = γ_i S₀ + Σ_{l≤i} (γ_i/γ_l) k_l ⊗ u_l\n```\n\nPseudo‑value `u_l = beta_l (v_l − k_lᵀ (g_l S_{l-1}))` depends on earlier `u_m (m<l)`, giving a\nlower‑triangular linear system solved once per chunk (the **UT / WY transform**, i.e. inverting\n`(I + tril(M,-1))` where `M_{lm} = beta_l (γ_l/γ_m)(k_l·k_m)`). Building blocks per chunk:\n\n1. **Preprocess**: `log g`, `beta`, within‑chunk cumulative `Γ = cumsum(log g)` (log space, f32).\n2. **Intra matrices**: decayed `A = tril((q k^T) or (k k^T) weighted by γ_i/γ_l)`.\n3. **UT transform**: solve the strictly‑lower‑triangular system to get `U` (and `W` for the state\n   contribution). Sizes are `[C, C]` and `[C, V]`, `[C, K]`.\n4. **Inter‑chunk scan** (sequential over chunks per (seq,head)): `S_new = γ_C S₀ + (K_scaled)ᵀ U`.\n5. **Output**: `o = scale * ( intra: tril‑masked decayed (q k^T) @ U  +  inter: γ ⊙ (q @ S₀) )`.\n\nParallelism: independent across `N × Hv` (= up to `34×8 = 272` programs for the largest workload)\nand across chunks *within* the intra‑chunk matmuls; the inter‑chunk scan is sequential per\n(seq, head). Compute is tiny (≈100k FLOP/token/head ⇒ ≈6.5 GFLOP at T=8192) — the op is\n**latency/dependency‑bound, not compute‑bound**, so kernel‑launch count and occupancy matter more\nthan raw MMA throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constraints (hard rules that shape the design)\n\nFrom `CLAUDE.md` / `TASK.md`:\n\n- **Triton‑only compute.** No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA‑extension/alternate‑impl fallback. A failing\n  Triton kernel is *invalid* — it may not be silently backed by a Torch path. PyTorch usage is\n  limited to tensor metadata, allocation, and launch plumbing (e.g. computing grids, `cu_seqlens`\n  handling, output allocation).\n- **Submission surface**: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(q, k, v, state, A_log, a, dt_bias, b,\n  cu_seqlens, scale)` returning `(output, new_state)` with the exact shapes/dtypes above.\n- **Immutable candidates.** Implement `c001`, `c002`, … sequentially, one source version at a time;\n  never reuse an ID for changed source; append one JSON record per evaluation to `candidates.jsonl`\n  without rewriting prior records.\n- **Evaluation.** Only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five fixed feedback\n  workloads together count as one evaluation. Budget: **100 evaluations**. Token soft/hard limits:\n  1.0M / 1.2M. `final` is operator‑only.\n- **Isolation.** Work only in this workspace; do not inspect parent dirs, baselines, evaluator,\n  controller, or other tasks. Permitted external knowledge sources are the `KernelWiki` and\n  `ncu-report-skill` skills only — but see §6 on their applicability here. No web/subagents/MCP.\n- **Do not run** CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, the external evaluator directly, or any alternate\n  correctness harness. (In this environment the general `Bash` tool is also disabled, so the *only*\n  sanctioned execution channel is the `evaluate_candidate.sh` launcher.)\n- **Metric.** Geometric‑mean speedup across selected workloads; **every** workload must pass\n  correctness or the candidate is invalid.\n\nLayout / semantics constraints to respect exactly:\n\n- State I/O is **k‑last `[N, H, V, K]`**; internally the recurrence is on `[K, V]`. Load with a\n  V↔K transpose relative to storage; store new_state transposed back. Getting the V/K axes swapped\n  is the single most likely functional bug — call it out in validation.\n- Output is over **8 v‑heads**; `q`/`k` are indexed with `head // 2` (GVA), never expanded in DRAM\n  unless deliberately chosen.\n- Output uses the **inclusive** (post‑update) state ⇒ diagonal‑inclusive causal mask.\n- `scale` multiplies only the final output (`q @ S_t`), not the state update.\n- Variable‑length batching: iterate sequences via `cu_seqlens`; sequences may be as short as a few\n  tokens (workloads have seqs down to ~10–35 tokens) and empty sequences (`seq_len ≤ 0`) must be\n  skipped (contribute nothing, but `new_state` still written — check reference: empty seq leaves\n  `new_state[seq]` as the zero‑init from `torch.zeros`, since the inner loop never runs and the\n  `state_HKV`... actually it `continue`s before writing → `new_state[seq]` stays **zeros**, not the\n  input state. Must replicate: for `seq_len ≤ 0`, `new_state[seq] = 0`, not the passed‑in state).\n\n### 2.1 Feedback workload profile\n\n| uuid (short) | T | N | avg tok/seq | regime |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1efaf2a9 | 42 | 2 | 21 | tiny — launch/latency bound |\n| d3dc3577 | 35 | 1 | 35 | tiny, single seq (only 8 programs) |\n| 1d0cc342 | 294 | 3 | 98 | small |\n| 15856e8c | 3028 | 5 | 606 | medium |\n| 5d3fc66a | 8192 | 34 | 241 | large (best parallelism: 272 seq·head programs) |\n\nImplications:\n- Two of five workloads are *tiny* (T=35, 42). There, fixed overheads (kernel launches, gate\n  precompute, `.item()` host syncs on `cu_seqlens`) dominate. Minimizing launch count and avoiding\n  host↔device syncs is as important as the main kernel. Prefer passing `cu_seqlens` to the kernel and\n  computing grids without per‑sequence `.item()` loops on the host.\n- The large workload rewards the chunked/MMA path and good occupancy; small ones reward low overhead.\n- A single immutable kernel must handle all five ⇒ favor a design whose chunk size / block sizes are\n  robust across `seq_len` from ~10 to ~600 (mask/pad partial chunks correctly).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Numerical risks\n\nThe reference computes **everything in float32** (bf16 operands cast up, f32 matmuls, f32 state).\nOutput is bf16, `new_state` is f32. Both are checked. Risks, ordered by importance:\n\n1. **State matmul precision (TF32 vs f32).** On Ampere, `tl.dot` with f32 operands defaults to TF32\n   (~10‑bit mantissa). The matmuls that touch the f32 state (`k @ old_state`, `q @ S₀`, and the\n   `Kᵀ @ U` state update) would then round f32 operands to TF32. Over up to `T/C ≈ 128` sequential\n   chunks the error in `new_state` (a checked f32 output) can accumulate. Mitigations to keep in the\n   toolbox: use `tl.dot(..., input_precision=\"ieee\")` (true f32, slower) or `\"tf32x3\"` (3‑pass, near\n   f32) for state‑touching matmuls; keep the state accumulator itself in f32 registers/SMEM and add\n   (not MMA) rank‑1 updates in the naive path. The bf16→f32 output has ~8‑bit mantissa, so TF32 is\n   *likely* fine for `output`; `new_state` is the tighter target.\n\n2. **bf16 operand matmuls are essentially exact vs reference.** `q,k,v` are already bf16 in DRAM, so\n   a bf16×bf16→f32 MMA computes the same products as the reference's f32 matmul of bf16‑origin\n   operands, only differing in accumulation order. So `q k^T`, `k k^T` intra‑chunk matmuls carry\n   negligible extra error — the precision worry is specifically the *f32* state operands (point 1).\n\n3. **Gate stability / log‑space accumulation.** Compute `log g = -exp(A_log)*softplus(x)` and do the\n   within‑chunk cumulative decay as a **cumsum in log space (f32)**, exponentiating ratios\n   `exp(Γ_i − Γ_l)` (always `≤ 0` exponent ⇒ `≤ 1`, no overflow; underflow to 0 is the physically\n   correct vanishing of old contributions). Use a numerically safe `softplus` (`log1p(exp(x))` with\n   the `x` large branch → `x`) to avoid `exp` overflow for large `x`.\n\n4. **UT transform conditioning.** The `(I + tril(M,-1))⁻¹` solve within a chunk is well‑conditioned\n   for reasonable `beta` and decay (strictly lower‑triangular nilpotent ⇒ the inverse is a finite\n   Neumann series of length `C`), but large `beta·(k·k)` products with slow decay can amplify error.\n   Solve in f32. Smaller chunk `C` (e.g. 32) reduces conditioning risk at some throughput cost.\n\n5. **Sigmoid/softplus in bf16 inputs.** `a`, `b` are bf16 — cast to f32 *before* `+dt_bias`,\n   `softplus`, `sigmoid` (reference does `a.float()`, `b.float()`). Doing gate math in bf16 would\n   diverge.\n\n6. **Empty / partial chunks & masking.** Partial last chunks (seq_len not divisible by `C`) must be\n   masked so out‑of‑range tokens contribute 0 to matmuls, the UT solve, and the state update.\n   Off‑by‑one in the inclusive‑diagonal mask flips the semantics.\n\n7. **Accumulation order across chunks.** The reference is a pure left‑to‑right scalar recurrence.\n   Chunking changes summation order; combined with TF32 this is the main source of `new_state`\n   drift. The **naive per‑token Triton path reproduces the exact recurrence order** and is the safest\n   correctness anchor.\n\n8. **Tolerances are not specified** in `definition.json`. Treat them as unknown; the official\n   evaluator is the source of truth (pass/fail per workload). Because I cannot run a local harness,\n   the first candidate must be the highest‑confidence (order‑preserving, f32) implementation to\n   establish that correctness passes at all, before trading precision for speed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\nPrimitives available on `sm_80`: bf16/f16 tensor cores (bf16×bf16→f32), TF32 for f32 MMA, up to\n~164 KB shared memory per SM (opt‑in), `tl.dot`, `tl.cumsum`, `tl.associative_scan`,\n`input_precision` control. No TMA/tcgen05/warp‑spec (those are Hopper/Blackwell).\n\nState footprint per (seq, head): `[K=128, V=128]` f32 = **64 KB**. This is the central resource\nconstraint: it fits in registers distributed across a warp‑group accumulator, or in SMEM, but limits\noccupancy. Splitting `V` (or `K`) into blocks (e.g. `BV=64`) halves the tile to 32 KB and raises the\nprogram count — a key tuning axis.\n\n### Axis A — algorithmic strategy\n\n- **A0. Naive per‑token recurrence (correctness anchor / first candidate).**\n  One program per `(seq, v_head)` (grid `N×8`). Keep `S = [128,128]` f32 in registers/SMEM; loop\n  over the sequence's tokens performing exact rank‑1 updates and the `q@S` output with f32 (no\n  tensor cores, or bf16 MMA only where exact). Reproduces the reference's order → highest correctness\n  confidence. Far faster than the Python reference (no host loop, no per‑token kernel launch), but\n  low arithmetic intensity and serial per head. Good baseline to *pass first*, then beat.\n\n- **A1. Chunked gated delta rule (UT/WY transform) — main performance target.**\n  Chunk size `C ∈ {16,32,64}`. Per (seq,head): preprocess gates, build decayed intra matrices, solve\n  the `[C,C]` UT transform, sequential inter‑chunk state scan, then intra+inter output. Uses tensor\n  cores for the `[C,·]` matmuls. Best throughput on the medium/large workloads. Higher\n  implementation and numerical risk (§3.1, §3.4). Typically several fused sub‑kernels.\n\n- **A2. Hybrid.** Naive per‑token for tiny sequences (T≤ threshold), chunked for the rest — but a\n  *single immutable kernel/config* must serve all five workloads, so any branch must be inside the\n  kernel or chosen by host‑side shape logic that is still one candidate. Keep as a later refinement.\n\n### Axis B — parallelization & tiling\n\n- Grid over `(seq, head)`; optionally add a `V`‑block (`BV`) axis for occupancy. The inter‑chunk scan\n  is sequential per (seq,head,V‑block); the `K` reduction is internal.\n- `BK`, `BV` block sizes (64 or 128); `C` chunk size; `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`; `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.\n- For tiny workloads, favor fewer/larger programs to cut launch overhead; for the large workload,\n  favor more programs (smaller `BV`) for occupancy. This tension is the main autotune target.\n\n### Axis C — precision knobs\n\n- `input_precision`/`allow_tf32` per matmul: bf16 operands → default; f32/state operands →\n  `\"ieee\"` or `\"tf32x3\"` (§3.1). Expose as a compile‑time constant to A/B against the evaluator.\n- Keep gate math and the log‑cumulative decay strictly in f32.\n\n### Axis D — layout & memory\n\n- Load `state` `[V,K]`→ transpose to `[K,V]` on load; store `new_state` transposed back. Use masked\n  loads for partial chunks and to zero empty sequences' `new_state`.\n- Avoid materializing GVA‑expanded `q_exp`/`k_exp` in DRAM: index `head//2` inside the kernel.\n- Compute `g`/`beta` either in a small fused preprocessing kernel over `[T,8]` or inline per chunk.\n  Inline avoids an extra pass but recomputes; a tiny separate pass is cheap and simplifies the main\n  kernel — decide by measurement.\n- Minimize host↔device syncs: pass `cu_seqlens` to the kernel and derive per‑program `(start, len)`\n  on device; avoid `.item()` per sequence (hurts the tiny workloads most).\n\n### Axis E — launch structure\n\n- Single fused main kernel vs. a small pipeline (gate precompute → chunk prep → scan → output).\n  Fewer launches help tiny workloads; a pipeline can raise throughput on the large one. Start fused\n  and simple; split only if profiling‑by‑evaluation (indirect) suggests it.\n\n### Candidate ladder (sequencing intent, not a plan)\n\n1. `c001` = A0 (order‑preserving f32 recurrence) — establish correctness passes on all five.\n2. Introduce chunking / tensor cores (A1) with conservative `C` and high‑precision state matmuls;\n   confirm correctness still passes, measure speedup.\n3. Tune precision knobs (Axis C), block sizes and chunk size (Axis B), and launch structure (Axis E)\n   for geomean, re‑validating correctness at each immutable step.\n4. Optionally hybridize (A2) for the tiny workloads if they dominate the geomean.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Validation strategy\n\n- **Source of truth = official evaluator only.** I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, or any local\n  correctness harness (both by rule and because `Bash` is disabled here). Each\n  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` runs all five workloads and consumes one of the\n  100 evaluations. Therefore: reason precisely on paper first; do not spend evaluations on\n  speculative variants.\n- **Correctness‑first ordering.** `c001` is deliberately the order‑preserving f32 recurrence so the\n  first evaluation answers \"does the pipeline wire up and pass tolerance at all?\" before any\n  precision/perf trade. Only after a green baseline do I introduce chunking and tensor cores.\n- **Guardrails I will self‑check before every evaluation** (cheap, catches the likely bugs):\n  1. State V↔K transpose on both load and store (k‑last `[N,H,V,K]` ⇄ internal `[K,V]`).\n  2. GVA indexing `q_head = k_head = h // 2` for `h ∈ [0,8)`.\n  3. Inclusive (diagonal) causal mask; output uses post‑update state; `scale` on output only.\n  4. Gate math in f32: `x=a.f32()+dt_bias`, `g=exp(-exp(A_log)*softplus(x))`, `beta=sigmoid(b)`.\n  5. Empty sequences (`seq_len ≤ 0`) ⇒ `new_state[seq] = 0` (matches reference `continue` before\n     writing), `output` untouched (zeros) for those tokens (there are none).\n  6. Output dtype bf16, `new_state` dtype f32; exact output/new_state shapes.\n  7. Partial‑chunk masking so padding contributes 0 to matmuls / UT solve / state update.\n  8. Handle `state is None` (all feedback workloads *do* pass a state, but keep the signature honest).\n- **Metric interpretation.** Rank by geometric‑mean speedup across the five workloads; a candidate is\n  only valid if *all five* pass. Because two workloads are tiny and two are medium/large, the geomean\n  balances launch overhead vs. throughput — I will watch that a throughput win on the large workload\n  doesn't regress the tiny ones (and vice versa).\n- **Evidence recording.** For every evaluated candidate append one complete JSON record to\n  `candidates.jsonl` with: parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation status, per‑workload result\n  (pass/fail + speedup), geomean, decision (keep/reject/branch), cumulative evaluation count, and\n  skill usage. Never rewrite earlier records.\n- **Convergence / stop.** Stop at the evaluation or token budget, or when successive immutable\n  candidates no longer improve geomean meaningfully; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n  Never run `final` without explicit operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Skill applicability note\n\n- **KernelWiki** targets NVIDIA **Blackwell (SM100/B200)** and **Hopper (SM90/H100)** techniques\n  (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/NVFP4/2‑SM, warp specialization, TMA, FA‑4, etc.). This task runs on **A800\n  (`sm_80`, Ampere)**, which has none of those features, so KernelWiki is out of scope and I do not\n  expect to invoke it; the relevant primitives here are Ampere bf16/TF32 tensor cores, SMEM, and\n  Triton scans. If a genuinely Hopper/Blackwell‑specific question arises it would not apply to this\n  hardware anyway.\n- **ncu-report-skill** requires running Nsight Compute on a B200 (`sm_100`) and profiling, which is\n  both hardware‑mismatched and forbidden here (\"do not run a profiler\"). I will therefore not use it;\n  performance signal comes indirectly from the official evaluator's measured speedups.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via the evaluator, minimizing spend)\n\n1. Exact correctness tolerance — inferred from whether `c001` (f32, order‑preserving) passes.\n2. What baseline the speedup is measured against (naive Python reference vs. an optimized kernel).\n   The Python‑loop reference is extremely slow, so even A0 should show large speedups; the geomean\n   scale will reveal the baseline's nature.\n3. Whether TF32 state matmuls stay within `new_state` tolerance, or `\"ieee\"`/`\"tf32x3\"` is required.\n4. Best `C`/`BV`/`num_warps`/`num_stages` per the workload mix, and whether the tiny workloads need a\n   low‑overhead path (A2) to protect the geomean.\n\n**Next step:** write `docs/plan.md` (executable plan), then implement `c001` as the order‑preserving\nf32 correctness anchor. Not done in this turn.\n","numLines":363,"startLine":1,"totalLines":363}}}
